 ## Overview
-CSG Operations on Implicit Functions combine multiple Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) into a single implicit function using Boolean operations: Union, Intersection, and Difference [S1][S2, S3, S7, unofficial][S8][S9, S10, S11, S13, S14, unofficial]. This method solves the problem of representing complex composite shapes from simple primitives by evaluating the resulting distance field directly, enabling rendering via ray marching or voxelization [S2, S7, S14, unofficial]. Unlike [SDF Domain Manipulation Operators](1730-sdf-domain-manipulation-operators.md), which modify the input position vector $\mathbf{p}$ to create periodic or mirrored structures, CSG operations modify the scalar distance values returned by base SDFs to define set-theoretic relationships between volumes [S1][S3, unofficial][S8].
+CSG Operations on Implicit Functions combine multiple Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) into a single implicit function using Boolean logic (Union, Intersection, Difference) to define complex composite shapes from simple primitives [S1][S2, S3, S7, unofficial]. Unlike sibling domain manipulation operators that transform the query position vector $\mathbf{p}$ to create periodic or mirrored structures [1730-sdf-domain-manipulation-operators.md], CSG operations act directly on the scalar distance values returned by child SDFs, reducing constructive solid geometry to arithmetic operations on scalar fields [S13, unofficial]. This method enables the rendering of complex geometries, such as near-tangent intersections, by evaluating the field functions of child objects and combining their density or distance values [S14, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method evaluates binary (or unary) operations on the scalar distance values $f(\mathbf{p})$ and $g(\mathbf{p})$ returned by two implicit functions at a query point $\mathbf{p}$ [S1][S3, unofficial][S8][S11, unofficial]. The core principle is that the sign of the SDF indicates inside ($<0$) or outside ($>0$) the volume, and the magnitude represents the distance to the surface [S3, unofficial][S8].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+- **Inputs:** Two or more SDF primitives (or composite SDFs), denoted as $f(\mathbf{p})$ and $g(\mathbf{p})$, which return the signed distance from a query point $\mathbf{p}$ to the surface. Negative values indicate the interior, positive values the exterior [S3, unofficial].
+- **Outputs:** A new SDF $h(\mathbf{p})$ representing the Boolean combination of the inputs.
 
-### Inputs and Outputs
